  "duration": 53.162,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.45,
      "text": "A minor planet barely 155 miles wide is wearing icy rings.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression close-up of the rings around Chariklo (eso1410b).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada/M. Kornmesser/Nick Risinger (skysurvey.org)",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_close-up_of_the_rings_around_Chariklo_(eso1410b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.567,
          "end": 5.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.45,
      "end": 12.57,
      "text": "For centuries, rings were only confirmed around giant planets: Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "PIA17172 Saturn eclipse mosaic bright crop.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA / JPL-Caltech / Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA17172_Saturn_eclipse_mosaic_bright_crop.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.467,
          "end": 8.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.233,
          "end": 12.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.57,
      "end": 20.39,
      "text": "In 2013, Brazilian astronomer Felipe Braga-Ribas watched the centaur Chariklo pass in front of a star.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.567,
          "end": 20.4,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.39,
      "end": 27.065,
      "text": "The starlight blinked briefly seconds before and after the rock passed, revealing two distinct rings.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "PIA17172 Saturn eclipse mosaic bright crop.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA / JPL-Caltech / Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA17172_Saturn_eclipse_mosaic_bright_crop.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.4,
          "end": 27.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 27.065,
      "end": 33.81,
      "text": "Down from planetary scales, these rings orbit only about 400 kilometers from Chariklo's center.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Centaur 10199 Chariklo (Illustration) (01GQJ8WWVBWDQV4P05Y74FVZRH).png",
        "credit": "ILLUSTRATION: NASA, ESA, CSA, Leah Hustak (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centaur_10199_Chariklo_(Illustration)_(01GQJ8WWVBWDQV4P05Y74FVZRH).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 27.067,
          "end": 30.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 30.833,
          "end": 33.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 33.81,
      "end": 39.905,
      "text": "Even wilder, the two icy bands measure just seven kilometers and three kilometers wide.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the view from inside the rings around Chariklo (eso1410c).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada/M. Kornmesser/Nick Risinger (skysurvey.org)",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_view_from_inside_the_rings_around_Chariklo_(eso1410c).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 33.8,
          "end": 36.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 36.667,
          "end": 39.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.905,
      "end": 46.25,
      "text": "That solved an old mystery: Chariklo dimmed for a decade when its rings tilted edge-on to Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Chariklo SDSS 2000-05-05 annotated.png",
        "credit": "Sloan Digital Sky Survey/Canadian Astronomy Data Centre",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chariklo_SDSS_2000-05-05_annotated.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.9,
          "end": 46.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 46.25,
      "end": 52.812,
      "text": "Astronomers hypothesize unseen shepherd moons keep them stable, preserving an impossible sight.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression close-up of the rings around Chariklo (eso1410b).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada/M. Kornmesser/Nick Risinger (skysurvey.org)",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_close-up_of_the_rings_around_Chariklo_(eso1410b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.267,
          "end": 53.167,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
